This confirms the problem.

Root cause

Your configured IPv6 address and the actual IPv6 address do not match.

Configured by MachineConfig (20-nodenet.conf)

CONTAINER_STREAM_ADDRESS=2405:100:200:72:881e:df7b:f42e:9621
KUBELET_NODE_IP=2405:100:200:72:881e:df7b:f42e:9621

Actual IPv6 addresses on the node

ens3f0:
2405:100:200:72::1:25

br-ex:
2405:100:200:72:50ed:ffbc:eea5:3135

The address:

2405:100:200:72:881e:df7b:f42e:9621

does not exist on the node, so CRI-O fails with:

bind: cannot assign requested address


---

Why did this happen?

The file:

/etc/systemd/system/crio.service.d/20-nodenet.conf

was generated by the Machine Config Operator. It should contain the node's current IP address.

Instead, it contains a stale IPv6 address that existed previously.

This is typically caused by one of the following:

the node's IPv6 address changed after reboot (SLAAC/DHCPv6),

a NetworkManager configuration changed,

or the Node Networking Operator (nodenet) generated the MachineConfig using an outdated address.



---

Let's find who generated it

Please run these commands from your bastion (using oc, since the API should be available once it comes back):

oc get machineconfig 99-master-generated-kubelet -o yaml

oc get machineconfig 01-master-kubelet -o yaml

oc get machineconfig rendered-master-ed7b2a32b2ffc9acc3a20f1cd1a1ad21 -o yaml

Also check:

oc get node sno1.lab1.ocp.lan -o yaml | grep -A5 addresses

I'm interested in what IPv6 address Kubernetes believes the node should have.


---

Temporary recovery (only if you need to bring the cluster up)

Since this is a MachineConfig-managed file, do not permanently edit it because the MCO will overwrite it.

However, to recover the cluster for troubleshooting, you can temporarily change:

vi /etc/systemd/system/crio.service.d/20-nodenet.conf

Replace:

CONTAINER_STREAM_ADDRESS=2405:100:200:72:881e:df7b:f42e:9621

with the actual interface address, for example:

CONTAINER_STREAM_ADDRESS=2405:100:200:72::1:25

Then reload and start CRI-O:

systemctl daemon-reload
systemctl restart crio
systemctl restart kubelet

This is only a temporary workaround to restore the node. The real fix is to identify why the MachineConfig generated the wrong 20-nodenet.conf.

One question

Before this reboot, was 2405:100:200:72:881e:df7b:f42e:9621 the node's IPv6 address?

If yes, then the reboot caused the host to obtain a new IPv6 address (2405:100:200:72::1:25), but the MachineConfig still references the old one. That would explain the behavior exactly.
